HALLANDALE -- Philippe Genderon misses his weekly dose of murder, mayhem and mishap, Canadian style.
He and his French Canadian friends here have to settle for American game shows on television -- which, by comparison, are dull, dull, dull.
"We don't know what to do at night anymore, it's so bad," Genderon said. "American television is so boring for us. Yes, we watch Wheel of Fortune and Jeopardy, but it would be so much better if they were in French so we could understand exactly what is going on."
For two years, Genderon and other residents at two mobile home parks have been trying to get a permit from the city for a satellite dish to receive Canada's Radio Canada and Telemetropol television stations.
So far, they have failed, and that means no French-language news, no hockey games, and worst, no Le Temps D'Or, their favorite soap opera.
"We really want to see Le Temps D'Or because it has so much intrigue and love triangles in it," Genderon said. "That's a complicated soap opera that our women miss. It's pretty much like your Dynasty, and we love it."
The Canadian shows would be welcome at the 216 residences in the Sea Estas and Seville mobile home parks, which are about 90 percent Canadian.
But city officials say the satellite dish is not allowed because the parks' zoning was made more restrictive about three years ago, and structural additions are not allowed.
"The mobile home parks and anything that was on the homes before the zoning was passed can stay, of course, but no new improvements can be added," said zoning official Christy Dominguez.
Joseph Gauthier, manager at Seville, said the first question people moving into the park ask is: "Do you have satellite dishes for Canadian TV?"
"Sadly, I always have to say no," Gauthier said. "My gosh, there are so many French Canadians living at these parks that the first language here is French, but yet they can't watch their shows."
